    "Fix one value, and the problem becomes a two-number search for the rest.",
    "Sort first. For each index `i`, run two pointers on the part after it, looking for `-nums[i]`. Skip equal values to avoid duplicates.",
    "sort nums\nfor i in range(n):\n    if i > 0 and nums[i] == nums[i-1]: continue\n    lo, hi = i+1, n-1\n    while lo < hi:\n        s = nums[i] + nums[lo] + nums[hi]\n        if s == 0: record; lo += 1 while duplicate; hi -= 1 while duplicate\n        elif s < 0: lo += 1\n        else: hi -= 1",
    "Sorting is O(n log n) and each fixed `i` costs O(n) with two pointers, so O(n^2) total versus O(n^3) brute force. Because the array is sorted, triplets come out in sorted order and skipping repeats removes duplicates."
  ],
